---
/*
 * cbf-libs-line-editor — the side sheet where a LIB cost line is created and
 * edited on /lib-entry-sheet. The counterpart to the read-only ledger in
 * cbf-libs-cost-table.
 *
 * ── WHY A SHEET, AND WHAT IT BUYS ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * The inline grid (/lib-entry) has one real cost: a cell is not wide enough to
 * hold a label. Every control there is captioned by a column header nine
 * categories have to share, or by an aria-label nobody sees. This surface has
 * room, so every field gets a REAL label, its own help text, and its own error —
 * which is what makes a rule like "the unit decides what the rate means" or "an
 * override above the ceiling is the failure" sayable in the place the vendor is
 * about to break it, rather than as a note squeezed under a 76px box.
 *
 * The trade is equally real and belongs in this comment: you can no longer see
 * the salary line while you edit the phone line whose months follow it. That is
 * why the sheet prices a LIVE PREVIEW against the whole budget — the amount, the
 * fringe it carries, and every gate recompute as you type, so the dependency is
 * still legible even though its other end is behind the backdrop.
 *
 * ── ONE FORM, NOT FIVE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every field of every shape ships in one form and the draft reveals what it
 * owns — the same device the entry grid uses per row, and for the same reason:
 * changing a vehicle kind from a schedule-rated lease to a typed premium, or
 * moving a misfiled item across the capitalization threshold, changes the
 * shape of the line WITHOUT the vendor leaving the sheet.
 *
 * ── NOTHING IS COMMITTED UNTIL SAVE ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * The sheet edits a DRAFT — a copy. Cancel leaves no trace, which is the one
 * thing an inline appended row could never quite manage (it had to be created
 * before it could be typed into, so abandoning it meant deleting it). Save
 * hands a finished line back to the ledger, which owns the array.
 *
 * ── THE COR LENS IS A THIRD MODE, NOT A DISABLED FORM ───────────────────────
 * A reviewer does not type into someone else's budget, so `review` mode renders
 * the line as FACTS and gives the sheet a different job: writing the mark. That
 * is an edit too — of the review, not of the line — and it belongs on the same
 * surface, next to the numbers it is about.
 *
 * ── EVENT CONTRACT (all on `document`) ──────────────────────────────────────
 * LISTEN   'cbf-libs:open-editor'
 *   detail: { mode: 'create'|'edit'|'review', draft, lines, cfg, scope, review }
 *   `lines` is the ledger's live array — read here to price the preview, NEVER
 *   mutated. `draft` is copied on receipt.
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-libs:save-line'   detail: { line }   // a finished line
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-libs:delete-line' detail: { id }
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-lib:review'       detail: { review } // a mark was written
 *
 * bcn-lego-checked: the drawer IS esa-side-dialog — I read its source (own
 * backdrop, focus trap, Esc-to-close, focus restore, symmetric slide-out), and
 * it is the lego the spoke already uses for cbf-lib-subcontract-sheet. Every
 * control is a lego: esa-text-field, esa-select, esa-combobox (the GSA schedule
 * is thousands of rows, so it is SEARCHED), esa-button, esa-radio-group,
 * esa-textarea, esa-badge, esa-alert-box, esa-field-error. The work-element
 * allocation is a labelled esa-text-field per element laid out with .repel —
 * the ecology catalog was re-walked and has no percentage-split or
 * key-value-editor lego. Beacon is not cloned on this machine.
 */
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import EsaAlertBox from '@esa/ecology/esa-alert-box.astro';
import EsaFieldError from '@esa/ecology/esa-field-error.astro';
import { workElements, weLabel,
  equipmentClassLabel,
} from '../../data/lib-entry.mjs';
---

<esa-side-dialog size="lg" position="right" heading="Line" data-libs-editor>
  <div class="cbf-lse stack" data-gap="lg">
    <!-- Where this line sits in the budget. On create it is the answer the vendor
         already gave by choosing a menu item; on edit it is the thing the
         capitalization gate can change out from under them, so it is stated. -->
    <p class="cbf-lse__scope type-body-small" data-scope></p>

    <!-- ── 1 · What this line IS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
         Four categories know the name better than the vendor does, so the
         description is replaced by the control that actually decides it. One of
         these four is revealed; the rest are inert. -->
    <section class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-group="identity">
      <h3 class="cbf-lse__h3 type-card-title">The line</h3>

      <esa-text-field
        data-field="label"
        data-ident="label"
        size="md"
        label="Description"
        placeholder="Describe this cost line"
        help-text="What a reviewer will see in the ledger."
      ></esa-text-field>

      <!-- ── The position: typed here, referenced everywhere after ──────────
           Positions are defined BY this LIB, not chosen from a list BPA
           supplies. It is an autocomplete rather than a plain text box because
           the SECOND line for a position must reuse the first one's title
           exactly — retyping it invites the near-miss ("Seasonal Tech") that
           would silently split the position in two and generate it a second
           fringe line. So the titles already on this budget are offered, and a
           term matching none of them offers to become a new one. -->
      <span data-ident="position" class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
        <esa-combobox
          data-position-combo
          mode="autocomplete"
          size="md"
          label="Position title"
          placeholder="Type a position title"
          help-text="Titles, never names. Typing a new title is what defines the position."
        ></esa-combobox>
        <span data-position-err hidden>
          <EsaFieldError
            message="Name the position this line pays for — the fringe line and any line that follows this position refer back to it."
            icon
          />
        </span>
      </span>

      <esa-select
        data-travel-kind-select
        data-ident="travel-kind"
        size="md"
        label="Travel kind"
        placeholder="Pick a travel kind"
        help-text="The kind fixes the units and the slice of the GSA schedule this line may reference."
      ></esa-select>

      {/* ── Which journey this cost belongs to, if any ────────────────────────
          On the ledger, grouping is a drag. Here it is a question, because this
          surface's bet is that every fact about a line is asked in a labelled
          field with room to explain itself — and "is this part of a trip" is a
          fact about the line, not a layout of the table. Answering "not part of
          a trip" is what makes the destination this line's own business, which
          is why the two controls sit together. */}
      <span class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-travel-group hidden>
        <esa-select
          data-trip-select
          size="md"
          label="Trip"
          placeholder="Not part of a trip"
          help-text="A trip carries the destination and how many times it happens for every line under it. A one-off cost has neither to say — leave it out of every trip."
        ></esa-select>

        <span data-travel-dest hidden>
          <esa-text-field
            data-field="locality"
            size="md"
            label="Destination"
            placeholder="City, ST"
          ></esa-text-field>
        </span>
      </span>

      <esa-select
        data-vehicle-kind-select
        data-ident="vehicle-kind"
        size="md"
        label="Vehicle kind"
        placeholder="Pick a vehicle kind"
        help-text="The kind decides whether the rate is referenced from the schedule or quoted."
      ></esa-select>

      {/* No second line under the identity control: the kind's rule is the kind
          picker's own help text, and the free-text detail it also carried had no
          input anywhere. */}
    </section>

    <!-- ── 2 · How much of it ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-group="quantity">
      <h3 class="cbf-lse__h3 type-card-title">Quantity</h3>

      <!-- One row, because it is one multiplication: the quantity, its unit, and
           the second quantity that multiplies it. A second row put a line break
           where the × belongs, which reads as two separate questions. Same fix as
           the entry grid's quantity cell — the row wraps on a narrow sheet rather
           than being pre-broken. -->
      <div class="cbf-lse__row" data-qty-typed>
        <esa-text-field data-field="qty" size="md" label="Quantity"></esa-text-field>
        <esa-text-field data-field="qtyUnit" data-unit-open size="md" label="Unit" placeholder="unit"></esa-text-field>
        <esa-select data-time-unit-select data-unit-select size="md" label="Time unit"></esa-select>
        <p class="cbf-lse__static" data-unit-static hidden>
          <span class="cbf-lse__static-label type-body-small">Unit</span>
          <span class="cbf-lse__static-value type-body" data-unit-static-value></span>
        </p>

        <esa-text-field data-field="qty2" data-qty2 size="md" label="Second quantity" hidden></esa-text-field>
        <esa-text-field data-field="qty2Unit" data-unit2-open size="md" label="Unit" placeholder="unit" hidden></esa-text-field>
        <p class="cbf-lse__static" data-unit2-static hidden>
          <span class="cbf-lse__static-label type-body-small">Unit</span>
          <span class="cbf-lse__static-value type-body" data-unit2-static-value></span>
        </p>
      </div>
      <!-- No "add a second quantity" control: the categories that offered it —
           Supplies, Meetings, Rent — are all quantity × cost EACH, so a second
           multiplicand had nothing to mean. The second quantity that survives is
           the one a vehicle KIND supplies, which knows both units. -->

      <!-- A travel line is quantified by its TRIP as much as by itself: the trip
           supplies the occurrences, this line supplies what is counted and for
           how long. Both units are fixed by the kind. -->
      <div class="cbf-lse__row" data-travel-qty hidden>
        <esa-text-field data-field="count" size="md" label="Count"></esa-text-field>
        <esa-text-field data-field="duration" data-duration size="md" label="Duration"></esa-text-field>
      </div>

      <p class="cbf-lse__static" data-qty-derived hidden>
        <span class="cbf-lse__static-label type-body-small">Quantity</span>
        <span class="cbf-lse__static-value type-body" data-qty-derived-value></span>
        <span class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-qty-derived-src></span>
      </p>

      <p class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-qty-void hidden>
        This shape has no quantity — the amount is entered directly.
      </p>

      <!-- The contract period is a ceiling on ONE individual's time, and it is
           arithmetic, so the form does it rather than leaving it to the reviewer. -->
      <div data-gate="months" hidden>
        <EsaAlertBox variant="danger" title="Past contract period">
          <span data-gate-msg="months"></span>
        </EsaAlertBox>
      </div>
      <p class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-months-note hidden></p>

    </section>

    <!-- ── 3 · What it costs ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <section class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-group="rate">
      <h3 class="cbf-lse__h3 type-card-title">Rate</h3>

      <esa-text-field data-field="rate" data-rate-typed size="md" prefix="$" label="Cost per unit"></esa-text-field>

      <!-- Shape D: one dollar amount, no quantity and no rate. -->
      <esa-text-field
        data-field="amount"
        data-rate-lump
        size="md"
        prefix="$"
        label="Amount"
        help-text="A lump sum — no quantity, no rate."
      ></esa-text-field>

      <!-- ── Claiming a published standard rate ──────────────────────────────
           A travel rate is the vendor's own number. Three kinds have a published
           standard they may claim instead, and claiming it takes two answers of
           its own — which location, which fiscal year — because those are what
           make the claim checkable a year later, when the schedule has moved and
           the budget has not. The claim supersedes the typed rate without
           erasing it, which is why the field above stays filled. -->
      <div class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-standard hidden>
        <span data-standard-toggle>
          <EsaButton size="xs" color="secondary" appearance="soft">Use the standard rate</EsaButton>
        </span>

        <div class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-standard-fields hidden>
          <span data-standard-where hidden>
            <esa-text-field
              data-field="standardLocality"
              size="md"
              label="Rate location"
              placeholder="City, ST"
              help-text="Which locality's published rate this line is claiming. Seeded from the destination, then it is yours."
            ></esa-text-field>
          </span>
          <esa-select
            data-standard-year
            size="md"
            label="Rate year"
            placeholder="Pick a fiscal year"
            help-text="Published rates change every 1 October. A claim says which year's schedule it is claiming, so it stays checkable after the schedule moves."
          ></esa-select>
          <p class="cbf-lse__static">
            <span class="cbf-lse__static-label type-body-small">Standard rate</span>
            <span class="cbf-lse__static-value type-body" data-standard-rate></span>
            <span class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-standard-src></span>
            <span class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-standard-superseded></span>
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-standard-none hidden></p>
      </div>

      <!-- Referenced, not typed. A combobox because the real schedule is
           thousands of localities: the row has to be SEARCHED for, which is also
           the honest argument for why this number is not an open input. -->
      <div class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-rate-gsa hidden>
        <esa-combobox
          data-gsa-select
          size="md"
          label="GSA schedule row"
          placeholder="Select a schedule row"
          search-placeholder="Search locality or rate type…"
        ></esa-combobox>
        <p class="cbf-lse__static">
          <span class="cbf-lse__static-label type-body-small">Scheduled rate</span>
          <span class="cbf-lse__static-value type-body" data-ref-rate></span>
          <span class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-ref-src></span>
        </p>
        <span data-override-toggle>
          <EsaButton size="xs" color="secondary" appearance="soft">Override rate</EsaButton>
        </span>
        <esa-text-field
          data-field="rateOverride"
          data-override-field
          size="md"
          prefix="$"
          label="Overridden rate"
          help-text="A rate above the schedule ceiling has to be justified in the narrative."
          hidden
        ></esa-text-field>
        <div data-gate="gsa" hidden>
          <EsaAlertBox variant="danger" title="Over GSA ceiling">
            <span data-gate-msg="gsa"></span>
          </EsaAlertBox>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Fringe is not a budgeting choice — it is the organization's rate
           applied to a position. So it is one pre-filled field on the line that
           causes it, and the note says what it just added to the Fringe section. -->
      <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-fringe hidden>
        <esa-text-field
          data-field="fringePct"
          size="md"
          suffix="%"
          label="Fringe rate"
        ></esa-text-field>
        <p class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-fringe-note></p>
      </div>

      <!-- The capitalization threshold FILES the item rather than grading it, so
           the ordinary state is a note: the per-item cost, the section it put the
           item in, and what that section does to the money. The alert is raised
           only for what the sort cannot settle — an override, or a lump with no
           per-item cost to sort on. The override toggle sits OUTSIDE the alert,
           because turning one on is done from the passing state. -->
      <div class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-gate="capital" hidden>
        <EsaAlertBox variant="warning" title="Capitalization threshold">
          <span data-gate-msg="capital"></span>
        </EsaAlertBox>
      </div>
      <p class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-cap-note hidden></p>
      <span data-cap-move hidden>
        <EsaButton size="sm" color="secondary" appearance="soft">Override the threshold</EsaButton>
      </span>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 4 · Which work it pays for ────────────────────────────────────────
         In the grid this was a popover of six cramped percentage boxes. Here it
         is a labelled field per work element with the element's full name — the
         thing the popover could never fit. -->
    <section class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-group="allocation">
      <h3 class="cbf-lse__h3 type-card-title">Work-element allocation</h3>
      <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
        {workElements.map((we: any) => (
          <div class="cbf-lse__alloc repel" data-gap="sm">
            <span class="cbf-lse__alloc-label type-body">{weLabel(we.id)}</span>
            <esa-text-field
              class="cbf-lse__alloc-input"
              data-alloc-we={we.id}
              size="md"
              suffix="%"
              aria-label={`${weLabel(we.id)} percent`}
            ></esa-text-field>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="cbf-lse__alloc-sum">
        <span class="type-body-small">Allocated</span>
        <strong data-alloc-sum>0%</strong>
        <span class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small">of 100%</span>
      </p>
      <span data-alloc-err hidden>
        <EsaFieldError message="" icon />
      </span>
      <p class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-alloc-derived hidden></p>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 5 · The COR's lens: the line as facts, and the mark ───────────────
         A reviewer reads the same numbers the vendor typed and writes the one
         thing they own — what has to change, on the line it is about. -->
    <section class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-group="facts" hidden>
      <h3 class="cbf-lse__h3 type-card-title">The line</h3>
      <dl class="cbf-lse__facts stack" data-gap="2xs" data-facts></dl>
    </section>

    <section class="stack" data-gap="sm" data-group="mark" hidden>
      <h3 class="cbf-lse__h3 type-card-title">Mark this line for the vendor</h3>
      <esa-radio-group data-mark-sev size="md" label="How hard is this?"></esa-radio-group>
      <esa-select data-mark-reason size="md" label="Reason" placeholder="Pick a reason"></esa-select>
      <esa-textarea
        data-mark-note
        label="What has to change"
        rows="4"
        auto-resize
        help-text="Written to the vendor. Say what to do, not just what is wrong."
      ></esa-textarea>
    </section>

    <!-- ── 6 · The number this whole sheet is about ─────────────────────────
         Live, and priced against the WHOLE budget rather than against the draft
         alone — a fringe base and a linked quantity are not knowable from one
         line. This is what pays for the sheet: the dependency you can no longer
         see is still the dependency you can still read. -->
    <div class="cbf-lse__amount repel" data-gap="sm">
      <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
        <span class="cbf-lse__amount-label type-body">Amount</span>
        <span class="cbf-lse__note type-body-small" data-amount-note></span>
      </span>
      <strong class="cbf-lse__amount-value" data-amount>$0</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div slot="footer" class="repel" data-gap="sm">
    <span data-delete hidden>
      <EsaButton size="sm" color="danger" appearance="soft" icon="trash-2">Delete line</EsaButton>
    </span>
    <span data-mark-remove hidden>
      <EsaButton size="sm" color="ghost">Remove mark</EsaButton>
    </span>
    <span class="cluster" data-gap="sm">
      <span data-cancel>
        <EsaButton size="sm" color="secondary" appearance="outline">Cancel</EsaButton>
      </span>
      <span data-save>
        <EsaButton size="sm" color="primary">Save line</EsaButton>
      </span>
    </span>
  </div>
</esa-side-dialog>

<script>
  /* The shared view derivations. This surface kept local copies of most of
     them; the standard-rate helpers are bound from here instead, because a
     second copy of "what does this claim resolve to" is exactly the drift
     lib-entry-view exists to prevent. */
  import * as V from '../../lib/lib-entry-view';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-side-dialog';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-text-field';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-select';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-combobox';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-radio-group';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-textarea';
  import {
    SEVERITIES,
    REASONS,
    severityMeta,
    reviewMeta,
    loadReview,
    saveReview,
    flagFor,
    fireReview,
  } from '../../data/lib-review.mjs';
  import {
    SHAPES,
    categoryOf,
    priceLines,
    effectiveRate,
    effectiveQty,
    baseAmountFor,
    baseLabelFor,
    gsaRates,
    gsaRatesFor,
    gsaRatesForVehicleKind,
    gsaRate,
    isScheduleRated,
    weLabel,
    workElements,
    contract,
    contractMonths,
    rosterFor,
    positionKey,
    TIME_UNITS,
    timeUnit,
    isSalaryLine,
    timeInMonths,
    periodProblem,
    trips,
    tripLabel,
    NO_TRIP,
    isLooseTravel,
    TRAVEL_KINDS,
    travelKind,
    travelChain,
    travelCount,
    seedCountFor,
    travelLabel,
    travelLocality,
    hasStandardRate,
    standardNeedsLocality,
    defaultRateYear,
    fringeLabel,
    fringePctOf,
    fringeOnLine,
    isDerivedFringe,
    VEHICLE_KINDS,
    vehicleKind,
    perItemCost,
    onThreshold,
    thresholdTested,
    thresholdTargetFor,
    isClassOverride,
    canOverrideThreshold,
    autoFileEquipment,
    filingConsequence,
    countOf,
    indirectConfig,
    usd,
    qtyFmt,
    equipmentClassLabel,
} from '../../data/lib-entry.mjs';

  const dialog = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-libs-editor]') as any;
  if (dialog) init(dialog);

  function init(dialog: any) {
    const $ = <T extends HTMLElement = HTMLElement>(sel: string) => dialog.querySelector<T>(sel);
    const $$ = (sel: string) => Array.from(dialog.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(sel));

    /* ── Session state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       `draft` is a COPY — nothing the vendor types reaches the ledger until
       Save. `ledger` is the ledger's live array, read to price the preview and
       never written to. */
    let mode: 'create' | 'edit' | 'review' = 'edit';
    let draft: any = null;
    let ledger: any[] = [];
    let cfg: any = indirectConfig;
    let review: any = loadReview();

    const num = (s: any) => {
      const v = parseFloat(String(s ?? '').replace(/[^0-9.\-]/g, ''));
      return Number.isFinite(v) ? v : 0;
    };
    const cents = (n: number) => Math.abs((n ?? 0) % 1) > 0.0001;
    const money = (n: number) => usd(n, { cents: cents(n) });
    const numText = (n: number | null | undefined) =>
      n == null ? '' : cents(n) ? String(n) : n.toLocaleString('en-US');

    const show = (el: Element | null, on: boolean) => { if (el) (el as HTMLElement).hidden = !on; };
    const setText = (el: Element | null, text: string) => { if (el) el.textContent = text; };
    const setVal = (sel: string, v: string) => { const el: any = $(sel); if (el) el.value = v; };
    const setLabel = (sel: string, v: string) => { const el: any = $(sel); if (el) el.label = v; };
    const setBtnLabel = (wrap: Element | null, text: string) =>
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-button__label') ?? null, text);
    const setErrText = (wrap: Element | null, text: string) =>
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-field-error__text') ?? null, text);
    /** esa-alert-box renders <strong.esa-alert-box__title> + a slotted message. */
    const setGate = (key: string, on: boolean, title: string, msg: string) => {
      const wrap = $(`[data-gate="${key}"]`);
      show(wrap, on);
      if (!on || !wrap) return;
      setText(wrap.querySelector('.esa-alert-box__title'), title);
      setText($(`[data-gate-msg="${key}"]`), msg);
    };

    /* ── Derived reads — the same rules the ledger prints, against the draft ── */
    const displayLabel = (l: any): string =>
      l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? travelLabel(l)
      : l.category === 'personnel-fringe' ? fringeLabel(l, ledger)
      : l.category === 'personnel-salary' ? (l.label ?? '')
      : l.label ?? '';
    const identOf = (l: any): string =>
      l.category === 'personnel-salary' ? 'position'
      : l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? 'travel-kind'
      : l.category === 'vehicles' ? 'vehicle-kind'
      : 'label';

    /* Bound, not re-implemented — a category can now fix the unit too, and this
       was the fourth copy of the rule that decides it. */
    const unitLocked = V.unitLocked;
    const unitPicked = V.unitPicked;
    const lockedUnit = V.lockedUnit;
    const lockedUnit2 = V.lockedUnit2;
    const rateLabelOf = (l: any) => (isSalaryLine(l) ? timeUnit(l).rateLabel : 'Cost per unit');

    const claimsStandard = V.claimsStandard;
    const canClaimStandard = V.canClaimStandard;
    const standardNeedsWhere = V.standardNeedsWhere;
    const standardSrcText = V.standardSrcText;
    const typedSrcText = V.typedSrcText;
    const standardAmountText = V.standardAmountText;
    const supersededText = V.supersededText;
    const fiscalYearOptions = V.fiscalYearOptions;

    const gsaOf = (l: any) => gsaRate(l.gsaKey);
    const overridden = (l: any) => isScheduleRated(l) && l.rateOverride != null;
    const gsaOver = (l: any) => {
      const g = gsaOf(l);
      return !!(g && l.rateOverride != null && l.rateOverride > g.ceiling);
    };

    /* ── The capitalization threshold, on the sheet the numbers are typed on ──
       This is where the sort is most legible, because the per-item cost and the
       section it decides are the same view: type a rate and the scope line at the
       top of the sheet changes under you. What the gate raises is only what the
       sort cannot settle — an override, or a lump with no per-item cost. */
    const capLumpOpen = (l: any, a: any) =>
      onThreshold(l) && l.shape === SHAPES.LUMP && (a[l.id] ?? 0) >= contract.capitalizationThreshold;
    const capPass = (l: any, a: any) => !isClassOverride(l) && !capLumpOpen(l, a);

    const allocEntries = (l: any): [string, number][] =>
      (Object.entries(l.allocations ?? {}) as [string, number][]).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
    const allocSum = (l: any) => allocEntries(l).reduce((a, [, p]) => a + p, 0);

    /** Keep the stored label in step with a derived one — the review panel
        addresses lines BY NAME and must never quote a name the ledger stopped
        showing. */
    const syncLabel = (l: any) => {
      if (l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL || l.category === 'personnel-salary') l.label = displayLabel(l);
    };

    /** Where this line sits, as the sheet's subheading. Shared with the main
        grid's allocation sheet, which states the same fact about the same line —
        two copies of it is how the other view helpers drifted. */
    const scopeOf = V.scopeOf;

    /* ── The live preview ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Priced against the WHOLE budget with the draft swapped in, so a fringe
       base and a linked quantity resolve exactly as they will after Save. The
       ledger array is copied, never mutated. */
    const pricePreview = () => {
      const i = ledger.findIndex((l) => l.id === draft.id);
      const arr = i >= 0 ? ledger.map((l) => (l.id === draft.id ? draft : l)) : [...ledger, draft];
      return priceLines(arr, cfg);
    };

    /* ── Option sets ── */
    const gsaOptionsFor = (l: any) => {
      const rows =
        l.category === 'vehicles' && l.vehicleKind
          ? gsaRatesForVehicleKind(l.vehicleKind)
          : gsaRatesFor(l.category);
      return rows.map((g: any) => ({ label: `${g.location} · ${g.kind} · ${g.season}`, value: g.key }));
    };
    const positionOptions = () =>
      rosterFor(ledger).map((p: any) => ({ label: p.title, value: p.key }));
    /* esa-combobox only commits values present in `options`, so "create" is an
       OPTION rather than free text escaping the control: a term matching no
       existing title is offered as one. Its label contains the term, so it
       survives the combobox's own label filter. */
    const NEW_POS = '__new-position__:';
    const SEV_OPTIONS = [
      { label: severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].label, value: SEVERITIES.REQUIRED },
      { label: severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].label, value: SEVERITIES.QUESTION },
    ];
    const REASON_OPTIONS = REASONS.map((r: any) => ({ label: r.label, value: r.key }));

    /* Two answers, both the vendor's: WHERE the published rate is claimed for and
       WHICH fiscal year's schedule. The location seeds from wherever the line
       goes and is a separate value from that moment on — the destination names
       the journey, this prices the line. Mileage has no location: one national
       figure per year. */
    const normalizeClaim = (l: any, ref: any) => {
      const next: any = { year: Number(ref?.year) || defaultRateYear() };
      if (standardNeedsLocality(l.travelKind)) next.locality = ref?.locality ?? travelLocality(l) ?? '';
      return next;
    };

    /* ── A kind owns the units and the rate source, so changing it RESETS both ──
       Lifted verbatim in intent from the entry grid: a lodging line turned into a
       mileage line must not keep "2" and silently mean vehicles. */
    const applyTravelKind = (l: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = travelKind(kindKey);
      l.travelKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      l.duration = k.durationLabel ? (l.duration ?? 1) : undefined;
      l.count = seedCountFor(l.trip, kindKey, ledger.filter((x) => x.id !== l.id));
      /* Every travel rate is typed; the kind only decides whether a CLAIM on a
         published rate is available. A kind with nothing published drops a live
         claim, and the typed rate underneath is what the line falls back to. */
      l.rate = l.rate ?? 0;
      delete l.gsaKey;
      delete l.rateOverride;
      if (!hasStandardRate(kindKey)) delete l.standard;
      else if (l.standard) l.standard = normalizeClaim(l, l.standard);
      syncLabel(l);
    };

    const applyVehicleKind = (l: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = vehicleKind(kindKey);
      l.vehicleKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      l.shape = k.shape;
      l.qtyUnit = k.unit;
      if (k.secondUnit) { l.qty2 = l.qty2 ?? 1; l.qty2Unit = k.secondUnit; }
      else { delete l.qty2; delete l.qty2Unit; }
      if (k.shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY) {
        delete l.rate;
        const allowed = gsaRatesForVehicleKind(kindKey);
        if (!allowed.some((g: any) => g.key === l.gsaKey)) l.gsaKey = allowed[0]?.key ?? '';
      } else {
        delete l.gsaKey;
        delete l.rateOverride;
        l.rate = l.rate ?? 0;
      }
      if (!l.label) l.label = k.label;
    };

    /* ── fill: the entry VALUES, written only when the draft changes shape or on
       open — never while the vendor is typing, so the caret cannot jump. ── */
    const fill = () => {
      const l = draft;
      setVal('[data-field="label"]', l.label ?? '');
      setVal('[data-field="qty"]', numText(l.qty));
      setVal('[data-field="qtyUnit"]', l.qtyUnit ?? '');
      setVal('[data-field="qty2"]', numText(l.qty2));
      setVal('[data-field="qty2Unit"]', l.qty2Unit ?? '');
      setVal('[data-field="rate"]', numText(l.rate));
      setVal('[data-field="locality"]', l.locality ?? '');
      setVal('[data-field="standardLocality"]', l.standard?.locality ?? '');
      const yrSel: any = $('[data-standard-year]');
      if (yrSel) { yrSel.options = fiscalYearOptions(); yrSel.value = String(l.standard?.year ?? ''); }
      setVal('[data-field="rateOverride"]', numText(l.rateOverride));
      setVal('[data-field="amount"]', numText(l.amount));
      setVal('[data-field="count"]', numText(travelCount(l)));
      setVal('[data-field="duration"]', numText(l.duration));
      setVal('[data-field="fringePct"]', (fringePctOf(l) * 100).toFixed(1));

      $$('[data-alloc-we]').forEach((el: any) => {
        const p = l.allocations?.[el.dataset.allocWe];
        el.value = p != null ? String(p) : '';
      });

      const gsaSel: any = $('[data-gsa-select]');
      if (gsaSel) { gsaSel.options = gsaOptionsFor(l); gsaSel.value = l.gsaKey ?? ''; }
      const posCombo: any = $('[data-position-combo]');
      if (posCombo) { posCombo.options = positionOptions(); posCombo.value = l.staffKey ?? ''; }
      const tuSel: any = $('[data-time-unit-select]');
      if (tuSel) { tuSel.options = TIME_UNITS.map((u: any) => ({ label: u.plural, value: u.key })); tuSel.value = timeUnit(l).key; }
      const tkSel: any = $('[data-travel-kind-select]');
      if (tkSel) { tkSel.options = TRAVEL_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({ label: k.label, value: k.key })); tkSel.value = l.travelKind ?? ''; }
      /* "Not part of a trip" is an OPTION, not the absence of one: a select whose
         empty state means something has to say what it means. */
      const trSel: any = $('[data-trip-select]');
      if (trSel) {
        trSel.options = [
          { label: 'Not part of a trip', value: NO_TRIP },
          ...trips.map((t: any) => ({ label: tripLabel(t), value: t.key })),
        ];
        trSel.value = l.trip || NO_TRIP;
      }
      const vkSel: any = $('[data-vehicle-kind-select]');
      if (vkSel) { vkSel.options = VEHICLE_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({ label: k.label, value: k.key })); vkSel.value = l.vehicleKind ?? ''; }
    };

    /* ── paint: everything DERIVED — which fields exist, what they are called,
       every gate, and the live amount. Runs after every keystroke. ── */
    const paint = () => {
      const l = draft;
      /* File the draft BEFORE anything reads its category — the sort runs on the
         numbers currently in the form, so the scope line at the top of the sheet
         and the note beside the rate always agree with what was just typed. */
      autoFileEquipment(l, pricePreview().amounts);
      const priced = pricePreview();
      const a = priced.amounts;
      const reviewing = mode === 'review';

      dialog.heading =
        mode === 'create' ? 'Add line'
        : reviewing ? (displayLabel(l) || 'Untitled line')
        : `Edit — ${displayLabel(l) || 'Untitled line'}`;
      setText($('[data-scope]'), scopeOf(l));

      /* The COR reads facts and writes a mark; the vendor gets the form. */
      ['identity', 'quantity', 'rate', 'allocation'].forEach((g) =>
        show($(`[data-group="${g}"]`), !reviewing),
      );
      show($('[data-group="facts"]'), reviewing);
      show($('[data-group="mark"]'), reviewing);
      show($('[data-delete]'), mode === 'edit');
      show($('[data-mark-remove]'), reviewing && !!flagFor(review, l.id));
      setBtnLabel($('[data-save]'), reviewing ? 'Save mark' : mode === 'create' ? 'Add line' : 'Save line');

      if (reviewing) {
        paintFacts(a);
        paintAmount(l, a);
        return;
      }

      /* ── Identity: one of four controls names this line ── */
      const ident = identOf(l);
      $$('[data-ident]').forEach((el) => show(el, el.dataset.ident === ident));

      /* An untitled personnel line defines no position, so it prices nothing and
         generates no fringe. That is worth an error rather than a silent zero. */
      if (ident === 'position') {
        show($('[data-position-err]'), !positionKey(l.label ?? ''));
        /* The type is NOT restated here: it belongs to the position, and the
           sheet's scope subheading above already names the sub-block. */
      }

      /* ── Quantity: which of the four renderings this shape actually has ── */
      const isTravel = l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL;
      const voidQty = l.shape === SHAPES.LUMP || l.shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE;
      const derivedQty = !!l.qtyFrom;
      show($('[data-qty-typed]'), !isTravel && !voidQty && !derivedQty);
      show($('[data-travel-qty]'), isTravel);
      show($('[data-qty-derived]'), derivedQty);
      show($('[data-qty-void]'), voidQty);
      show($('[data-group="quantity"]'), !voidQty || isTravel);

      if (derivedQty) {
        const src = ledger.find((s) => s.id === l.qtyFrom);
        setText($('[data-qty-derived-value]'), `${qtyFmt(effectiveQty(l, ledger))} ${l.qtyUnit ?? ''}`.trim());
        setText($('[data-qty-derived-src]'), `Follows ${src ? displayLabel(src) : l.qtyFrom} — change it there.`);
      }

      /* Three renderings of the unit, exactly one live: open text where the
         category leaves it open, a SELECT on a personnel line (the unit must be
         time-bound, so it comes off a list rather than a keyboard), and a value
         where a vehicle kind already fixed it. */
      const locked = unitLocked(l);
      show($('[data-unit-open]'), !locked);
      show($('[data-unit-select]'), unitPicked(l));
      show($('[data-unit-static]'), locked && !unitPicked(l));
      setText($('[data-unit-static-value]'), lockedUnit(l));
      /* The unit decides what the number means, so it captions the number. */
      setLabel('[data-field="qty"]', isSalaryLine(l) ? `Time (${timeUnit(l).plural})` : 'Quantity');

      /* The second quantity and its unit are SIBLINGS in the quantity row now,
         not a row of their own — so the unit has to be gated on the quantity
         existing as well as on the unit being open. Nested, it inherited that
         for free; flattened, it would have shown an orphan unit box on every
         line with no second quantity. */
      const hasQty2 = l.qty2 != null && !isTravel && !voidQty;
      show($('[data-qty2]'), hasQty2);
      show($('[data-unit2-open]'), hasQty2 && !locked);
      show($('[data-unit2-static]'), hasQty2 && locked);
      setText($('[data-unit2-static-value]'), lockedUnit2(l));

      /* Grouping belongs to travel and to nothing else, and the destination
         belongs to a travel line only while it has no trip to inherit one from.
         The resolution — separately listed, or standard CONUS — rides the field
         as its help text, which is where this surface puts every other rule. */
      show($('[data-travel-group]'), isTravel);
      if (isTravel) {
        const loose = isLooseTravel(l);
        show($('[data-travel-dest]'), loose);
      }

      if (isTravel) {
        const k = travelKind(l.travelKind);
        setLabel('[data-field="count"]', k?.countLabel ? `Count (${k.countLabel})` : 'Count');
        show($('[data-duration]'), !!k?.durationLabel);
        setLabel('[data-field="duration"]', k?.durationLabel ? `Duration (${k.durationLabel})` : 'Duration');
      }

      /* The contract period, on the axis every time unit shares. */
      const monthsBad = isSalaryLine(l) && periodProblem(l, ledger) != null;
      setGate(
        'months',
        monthsBad,
        'Past contract period',
        `${countOf(effectiveQty(l, ledger), timeUnit(l).plural)} is ${qtyFmt(timeInMonths(l, ledger))} months of work in a ${contractMonths}-month contract period — one individual cannot be budgeted for more time than the contract runs.`,
      );
      show($('[data-months-note]'), isSalaryLine(l) && !monthsBad);
      if (isSalaryLine(l)) {
        setText(
          $('[data-months-note]'),
          `${countOf(effectiveQty(l, ledger), timeUnit(l).plural)} of the ${contractMonths}-month contract period.`,
        );
      }

      /* ── Rate: typed, claimed, referenced, or a single lump amount ──────
         A travel line always shows the typed field — disabled while a claim sits
         on top of it, because that number is still theirs and comes straight
         back when the claim is dropped. The GSA block below is vehicles only. */
      const isTravelLine = l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL;
      const scheduled = !isTravelLine && isScheduleRated(l);
      const claimed = claimsStandard(l);
      show($('[data-rate-typed]'), isTravelLine || (!scheduled && l.shape !== SHAPES.LUMP && l.shape !== SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE));
      const typedField: any = $('[data-rate-typed]');
      if (typedField) typedField.disabled = claimed;
      show($('[data-rate-lump]'), l.shape === SHAPES.LUMP);
      show($('[data-rate-gsa]'), scheduled);
      setLabel('[data-field="rate"]', rateLabelOf(l));

      /* The claim, and the two answers it is made of. */
      show($('[data-standard]'), isTravelLine);
      if (isTravelLine) {
        const offered = canClaimStandard(l);
        show($('[data-standard-toggle]'), offered);
        show($('[data-standard-fields]'), claimed);
        show($('[data-standard-where]'), claimed && standardNeedsWhere(l));
        show($('[data-standard-none]'), !offered);
        setText($('[data-standard-none]'), typedSrcText(l));
        setBtnLabel($('[data-standard-toggle]'), claimed ? 'Use my own rate' : 'Use the standard rate');
        setText($('[data-standard-rate]'), standardAmountText(l));
        setText($('[data-standard-src]'), standardSrcText(l));
        setText($('[data-standard-superseded]'), supersededText(l));
      }

      if (scheduled) {
        const g = gsaOf(l);
        setText($('[data-ref-rate]'), money(effectiveRate(l)));
        setText(
          $('[data-ref-src]'),
          g
            ? `Referenced from ${g.location} · ${g.kind} · ${g.season} — not typed. Ceiling ${money(g.ceiling)}.`
            : 'Pick the GSA schedule row this line references — it sets the rate.',
        );
        setBtnLabel($('[data-override-toggle]'), overridden(l) ? 'Use schedule rate' : 'Override rate');
        show($('[data-override-field]'), overridden(l));
        setGate(
          'gsa',
          gsaOver(l),
          'Over GSA ceiling',
          g
            ? `${money(l.rateOverride)} is over the ${g.kind} ceiling of ${money(g.ceiling)} for ${g.location} (${g.season}) — ${money(l.rateOverride - g.ceiling)} over.`
            : 'Select the GSA schedule row this line references.',
        );
      } else {
        setGate('gsa', false, '', '');
      }

      /* Fringe, stated where it is decided AND where it lands. */
      show($('[data-fringe]'), isSalaryLine(l));
      if (isSalaryLine(l)) {
        setText(
          $('[data-fringe-note]'),
          `${money(fringeOnLine(l, ledger, a))} of fringe, carried in Personnel — Fringe.`,
        );
      }

      /* The threshold: where this item just filed itself, and what that does to
         the money. The gate is raised only for what the sort could not settle. */
      const capBad = onThreshold(l) && !capPass(l, a);
      setGate(
        'capital',
        capBad,
        isClassOverride(l) ? 'Overrides the threshold' : 'Too large to test per item',
        isClassOverride(l)
          ? `Filed as ${equipmentClassLabel(l.block)} against the sort: at ${money(perItemCost(l, a))} per item the ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} threshold makes this ${equipmentClassLabel(thresholdTargetFor(l))}. This override needs the COR's agreement.`
          : `A ${money(a[l.id] ?? 0)} lump has no per-item cost, so the ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} threshold cannot reach inside it. Itemize this line — a quantity and a cost each — so each item files itself.`,
      );
      /* The move affordance is the OVERRIDE toggle now, and it runs one way: an
         item that met the threshold can be argued back down to a supply, and
         nothing argues a cheap item up. See canOverrideThreshold. */
      show($('[data-cap-move]'), canOverrideThreshold(l, a));
      setBtnLabel(
        $('[data-cap-move]'),
        isClassOverride(l) ? 'Use the threshold' : 'File as a supply instead',
      );
      show($('[data-cap-note]'), thresholdTested(l) && !capBad);
      if (thresholdTested(l)) {
        setText(
          $('[data-cap-note]'),
          `${money(perItemCost(l, a))} per item — filed as ${equipmentClassLabel(l.block)}. ${filingConsequence(l, indirectConfig)}`,
        );
      }

      /* ── Allocation ── */
      const sum = allocSum(l);
      setText($('[data-alloc-sum]'), `${sum}%`);
      const aErr = $('[data-alloc-err]');
      show(aErr, sum !== 100);
      if (sum !== 100) {
        setErrText(
          aErr,
          sum === 0
            ? 'No work element assigned — allocations must total 100%.'
            : `Allocated ${sum}% — work-element allocations must total 100%.`,
        );
      }

      paintAmount(l, a);
    };

    const paintAmount = (l: any, a: any) => {
      setText($('[data-amount]'), usd(a[l.id] ?? 0));
      setText(
        $('[data-amount-note]'),
        isSalaryLine(l)
          ? `Plus ${money(fringeOnLine(l, ledger, a))} of fringe in Personnel — Fringe.`
          : l.shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE
            ? `${money(baseAmountFor(l, ledger, a))} base · ${baseLabelFor(l, ledger)}`
            : 'Recomputed against the whole budget as you type.',
      );
    };

    /** The COR's read of the line: values, never disabled inputs. */
    const paintFacts = (a: any) => {
      const l = draft;
      const rows: [string, string][] = [];
      if (l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) rows.push(['Quantity', travelChain(l)]);
      else if (l.shape !== SHAPES.LUMP && l.shape !== SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE) {
        const one = `${qtyFmt(effectiveQty(l, ledger))} ${lockedUnit(l) || l.qtyUnit || ''}`.trim();
        rows.push(['Quantity', l.qty2 != null && !isSalaryLine(l) ? `${one} × ${qtyFmt(l.qty2)} ${l.qty2Unit ?? ''}`.trim() : one]);
      }
      if (l.shape !== SHAPES.LUMP) {
        const g = gsaOf(l);
        rows.push([
          isSalaryLine(l) ? timeUnit(l).rateLabel : 'Rate',
          g ? `${money(effectiveRate(l))} · ${g.location} · ${g.kind} · ${g.season}` : money(effectiveRate(l)),
        ]);
      }
      if (isSalaryLine(l)) {
        rows.push(['Fringe', `${(fringePctOf(l) * 100).toFixed(1)}% · ${money(fringeOnLine(l, ledger, a))}`]);
      }
      const e = allocEntries(l);
      rows.push(['Work element', e.length ? e.map(([id, p]) => `${weLabel(id)} — ${p}%`).join(', ') : 'Not allocated']);

      const dl = $('[data-facts]');
      if (!dl) return;
      dl.replaceChildren(
        ...rows.map(([k, v]) => {
          const row = document.createElement('div');
          row.className = 'cbf-lse__fact repel';
          row.dataset.gap = 'sm';
          const dt = document.createElement('dt');
          dt.className = 'type-body-small';
          dt.textContent = k;
          const dd = document.createElement('dd');
          dd.className = 'type-body';
          dd.textContent = v;
          row.append(dt, dd);
          return row;
        }),
      );
    };

    const fillMark = () => {
      const f = flagFor(review, draft.id);
      const sev: any = $('[data-mark-sev]');
      if (sev) { sev.options = SEV_OPTIONS; sev.value = f?.severity ?? SEVERITIES.REQUIRED; }
      const reason: any = $('[data-mark-reason]');
      if (reason) { reason.options = REASON_OPTIONS; reason.value = f?.reason ?? ''; }
      const note: any = $('[data-mark-note]');
      if (note) note.value = f?.note ?? '';
    };

    /* ── Open ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The draft is copied here — including its allocations map, which is the one
       nested object a shallow copy would leave shared with the ledger. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-libs:open-editor', (e: any) => {
      const d = e.detail ?? {};
      if (!d.draft) return;
      /* A derived fringe line has nothing on it a vendor decides; the ledger does
         not offer to open one, and this refuses in case some other caller does. */
      if (isDerivedFringe(d.draft) && d.mode !== 'review') return;

      mode = d.mode ?? 'edit';
      ledger = Array.isArray(d.lines) ? d.lines : [];
      cfg = d.cfg ?? indirectConfig;
      review = d.review ?? review;
      draft = { ...d.draft, allocations: { ...(d.draft.allocations ?? {}) } };

      fill();
      fillMark();
      paint();
      dialog.show();
    });

    /* esa-combobox re-queries on a debounced `search`. That is where the "use
       this new title" option is offered, because it depends on the term — the
       roster itself only ever holds titles already on this budget. */
    dialog.addEventListener('search', (e: any) => {
      const el = e.target as HTMLElement;
      if (!el?.dataset || !('positionCombo' in el.dataset)) return;
      const opts = positionOptions();
      const t = String(e.detail?.term ?? '').trim();
      const exists = opts.some((o: any) => o.label.toLowerCase() === t.toLowerCase());
      (el as any).options = !t || exists ? opts : [{ label: `Use “${t}”`, value: NEW_POS + t }, ...opts];
    });

    /* ── Every esa-* control fires a composed `change`; delegate the lot ── */
    dialog.addEventListener('change', (e: any) => {
      if (!draft) return;
      const el = e.target as HTMLElement;
      const d: any = el.dataset ?? {};

      if ('positionCombo' in d) {
        const picked = String((el as any).value ?? '');
        /* Two ways in, one outcome: pick a title this budget already defines, or
           commit the term typed into the box. Either way the line carries the
           TITLE and the key is derived from it — so a reused title lands on the
           same position without the vendor knowing a key exists at all. */
        const title = picked.startsWith(NEW_POS)
          ? picked.slice(NEW_POS.length).trim()
          : (rosterFor(ledger).find((r: any) => r.key === picked)?.title ?? '');
        draft.label = title;
        draft.staffKey = positionKey(title);
        /* The line keeps the sub-block it sits in — the block IS the type, and a
           position's type is a fact about the position, not about its title. */
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if ('timeUnitSelect' in d) {
        draft.timeUnit = (el as any).value;
        draft.qtyUnit = timeUnit(draft).plural;
        paint();
        return;
      }

      if ('travelKindSelect' in d) {
        applyTravelKind(draft, (el as any).value);
        fill();
        paint();
        return;
      }
      /* The two answers a claim is made OF — neither touches the trip's
         destination, which names the journey rather than pricing the line. */
      if (d.field === 'standardLocality') {
        if (!draft.standard) return;
        draft.standard = { ...draft.standard, locality: String((el as any).value ?? '').trim() };
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if ('standardYear' in d) {
        if (!draft.standard) return;
        draft.standard = { ...draft.standard, year: Number((el as any).value) || defaultRateYear() };
        paint();
        return;
      }

      /* ── Joining or leaving a trip ─────────────────────────────────────────
         The same repair the ledger's drag performs, asked as a question instead.
         Leaving carries the trip's destination away as the line's own — without
         it the line would silently re-price at CONUS — and joining drops that
         copy, because the trip head is then the only place it is typed. Either
         way the schedule row is re-pointed at wherever the line now goes. */
      if ('tripSelect' in d) {
        const picked = String((el as any).value ?? '');
        const toTrip = picked && picked !== NO_TRIP ? picked : null;
        if (toTrip) delete draft.locality;
        else draft.locality = travelLocality(draft);
        draft.trip = toTrip;
        draft.block = toTrip ?? NO_TRIP;
        if (travelKind(draft.travelKind)?.rateSource === 'gsa') {
          const match = gsaRowFor(draft.travelKind, travelLocality(draft));
          if (match) draft.gsaKey = match.key;
        }
        syncLabel(draft);
        fill();
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if ('vehicleKindSelect' in d) {
        applyVehicleKind(draft, (el as any).value);
        fill();
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if ('gsaSelect' in d) {
        draft.gsaKey = (el as any).value;
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if (d.allocWe) {
        const p = num((el as any).value);
        if (p > 0) draft.allocations[d.allocWe] = p;
        else delete draft.allocations[d.allocWe];
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if (!d.field) return;

      if (d.field === 'label') draft.label = (el as any).value ?? '';
      /* A destination is a place, not a number — and it decides three things:
         the schedule row this line references, the name it goes by, and the
         sentence under the field saying which of the two rate schedules the
         typed place landed on. */
      else if (d.field === 'locality') {
        /* Names the line. It prices nothing — a claim on a published rate carries
           its own location, stated once when the claim is made. */
        draft.locality = String((el as any).value ?? '').trim();
        syncLabel(draft);
      }
      else if (d.field === 'qtyUnit' || d.field === 'qty2Unit') draft[d.field] = (el as any).value ?? '';
      else if (d.field === 'fringePct') draft.fringePct = num((el as any).value) / 100;
      else draft[d.field] = num((el as any).value);
      paint();
    });

    dialog.addEventListener('click', (e: any) => {
      if (!draft) return;
      const t = e.target as HTMLElement;

      /* Claiming seeds both answers — location from wherever the line goes, year
         from the newest schedule carried — so the common case is one click. */
      if (t.closest('[data-standard-toggle]')) {
        if (draft.standard) delete draft.standard;
        else draft.standard = normalizeClaim(draft, {});
        fill();
        paint();
        return;
      }

      if (t.closest('[data-override-toggle]')) {
        if (draft.rateOverride == null) draft.rateOverride = gsaRate(draft.gsaKey)?.rate ?? 0;
        else delete draft.rateOverride;
        fill();
        paint();
        return;
      }
      /* Overruling the sort, and handing the line back to it. The sheet stays
         open on the same line either way — the item never had to be re-typed
         somewhere else, which is what the old "move category" button was for. */
      if (t.closest('[data-cap-move]')) {
        if (isClassOverride(draft)) {
          delete draft.classOverride;
          delete draft.filedAtThreshold;
        } else {
          /* One direction only: an item that met the threshold is argued back
             down to a supply. Same section either way — only the block moves. */
          draft.classOverride = true;
          draft.block = 'expensed';
        }
        paint();
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-cancel]')) {
        dialog.close();
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-delete]')) {
        document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cbf-libs:delete-line', { detail: { id: draft.id } }));
        dialog.close();
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-mark-remove]')) {
        review.flags = (review.flags ?? []).filter((f: any) => f.lineId !== draft.id);
        review.addressed = (review.addressed ?? []).filter((id: string) => id !== draft.id);
        saveReview(review);
        fireReview(review);
        dialog.close();
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-save]')) {
        if (mode === 'review') saveMark();
        else saveLine();
      }
    });

    const saveLine = () => {
      syncLabel(draft);
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cbf-libs:save-line', { detail: { line: draft } }));
      dialog.close();
    };

    const saveMark = () => {
      const flag = {
        lineId: draft.id,
        severity:
          ($('[data-mark-sev]') as any)?.value === SEVERITIES.QUESTION
            ? SEVERITIES.QUESTION
            : SEVERITIES.REQUIRED,
        reason: ($('[data-mark-reason]') as any)?.value || 'other',
        note: String(($('[data-mark-note]') as any)?.value ?? '').trim(),
        author: reviewMeta.reviewer,
        date: reviewMeta.today,
      };
      const i = (review.flags ?? []).findIndex((f: any) => f.lineId === draft.id);
      if (i >= 0) review.flags[i] = flag;
      else review.flags = [...(review.flags ?? []), flag];
      /* Re-marking a line the vendor already ticked off puts it back on the list —
         the mark changed, so "addressed" is no longer a claim about this mark. */
      review.addressed = (review.addressed ?? []).filter((id: string) => id !== draft.id);
      saveReview(review);
      fireReview(review);
      dialog.close();
    };

    /* The panel and this sheet both write the review; keep the local copy current
       so a mark written elsewhere is the one this sheet opens on. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:review', (e: any) => {
      const incoming = e.detail?.review;
      if (incoming && incoming !== review) review = incoming;
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* The sheet is a form, so it is typed as one: full-strength labels from the
     legos, and quiet 13px second lines that DARKEN rather than gray out. */
  .cbf-lse {
    padding-block-end: var(--spacing-200);
  }
  .cbf-lse__h3 {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }
  .cbf-lse__scope {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lse__note {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    max-width: 56ch;
  }

  /* Fields that qualify one another sit on one line and wrap together — a
     quantity and its unit are one answer, not two. */
  .cbf-lse__row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .cbf-lse__row > * { flex: 1 1 8rem; min-width: 0; }


  /* Layout primitives set display:flex, which beats the UA [hidden] rule at equal
     specificity — and this whole form works by revealing one of several
     renderings. Without this, every shape's fields would show at once. */
  [hidden] { display: none !important; }

  /* A value the vendor did NOT type — referenced or fixed by a kind. Rendered as
     a fact with its own caption, never as a disabled input: a grayed-out field
     reads as "you failed to fill this in", which is a lie about a value nobody
     was ever asked for. */
  .cbf-lse__static {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-050, 2px);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cbf-lse__static-label { color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .cbf-lse__static-value {
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* ── Work-element allocation ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     .repel wraps by default, which sends the percent box onto its own line the
     moment a work-element name is long — and these names ARE long ("C. 165
     Produce Environmental Compliance Documentation"). Six rows where some have
     their input beside the label and some below it reads as six different
     controls. So the row does not wrap: the NAME wraps inside its own column and
     the box stays put, which is what makes the column of boxes scannable. */
  .cbf-lse__alloc { --align: center; align-items: center; flex-wrap: nowrap; }
  .cbf-lse__alloc-label {
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .cbf-lse__alloc-input { width: 7rem; flex: none; }
  .cbf-lse__alloc-sum {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
    margin: 0;
    padding-block-start: var(--spacing-200);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border-light);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lse__alloc-sum strong {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* ── The COR's read-only facts ──
     Only the <dl> is authored markup, so only it can be styled from this scoped
     block. The .cbf-lse__fact rows are built at runtime (fillFacts, above), so
     their rules live in the is:global block at the end of this file. */
  .cbf-lse__facts { margin: 0; }

  /* ── The number the sheet is about ──────────────────────────────────────── */
  .cbf-lse__amount {
    --align: center;
    align-items: center;
    padding: var(--spacing-300);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-surface-sunken);
  }
  .cbf-lse__amount-label {
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
  }
  .cbf-lse__amount-value {
    font-size: var(--type-size-500);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The sheet is wider than the default md drawer because a labelled form with
     a live amount needs the room — that room IS the argument for this version. */
  esa-side-dialog[data-libs-editor] {
    --side-dialog-width-lg: 34rem;
  }
</style>

<!-- The fact rows are created by document.createElement in the script above, so
     they never carry Astro's scope hash — a scoped rule would silently miss them.
     Global, but every selector is anchored to this component's root class. -->
<style is:global>
  .cbf-lse .cbf-lse__fact { --align: baseline; align-items: baseline; }
  .cbf-lse .cbf-lse__fact dt { color: var(--color-text-secondary); min-width: 0; }
  .cbf-lse .cbf-lse__fact dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    text-align: end;
  }
</style>
